# Exhaustiveness: the summary block must be EXACTLY this. Any extra
# application section or changed resource fails here rather than being
# quietly pinned by the next --regenerate.
expect:affected=1 changed=1 resources=+0,-0,~1
# A SECOND lint tool must reach the report through the same contract: this
# finding exists only when argocdf ran the case's extra --lint command
# (scripts/lint-conftest.sh, added for this case alone via the harness's
# CASE_ARGS) and kept its stdout line. conftest is OFFLINE, so unlike
# lint-cr-policy this proves nothing about cluster propagation - only that the
# lint contract is tool-agnostic.
must:\[target\] \[lint#2/no_plaintext_credentials\] ConfigMap/cluster-info-cm
# Base still carries the clean note, so the finding is target-only. This also
# fails if the adapter reports the same document twice (once per side).
must-not:\[base\] \[lint#2/
# The values edit itself must reach the rendered ConfigMap - a finding without
# it would mean conftest linted something other than this render.
must:^\+.*note:.*password=placeholder
# The row's headline is that kyverno ALSO ran and contributed nothing - empty output
# at exit 0 is "no findings", not a failure. Nothing guarded it: the historical
# empty-stdout-as-failure bug would have added kyverno lint-failure warnings and every
# check above would still pass. So no FINDING from lint#1 (this case's kyverno
# adapter, whose bracket would continue into a policy name)...
must-not:\[lint#1/
# ...and no failure line from EITHER adapter. Those are the lines whose bracket ENDS
# at the linter, followed by the failure itself - which is what distinguishes them
# from the conftest finding this case requires above, whose bracket continues into a
# policy name. A must-not on the bare prefix would forbid that finding too, and the
# case could never pass.
must-not:\[lint#[0-9]+\] (exit status|timeout after|signal:)
# ...and the absence must be an absence of FINDINGS, not of the invocation: a
# report cannot distinguish "kyverno ran and matched nothing" from "kyverno was
# never invoked" (both are empty by the exit-code contract), so the fresh run's
# LOG must show the kyverno adapter invoked and contributing zero lines. Checked
# by run.sh at run time; argocdf logs one line per linter invocation. status=ok is
# part of the pattern on purpose: lines=0 ALONE is also what a skipped linter logs,
# so without it this check would pass for an adapter that never ran - the exact
# thing it exists to rule out. lint#1 is the first --lint of this case's CASE_ARGS
# (the kyverno adapter); lint#2 is the conftest one. BOTH sides are asserted, and the
# app is named: a single pattern without them passed on the target-side record alone,
# so a regression that skipped base-side linting entirely still satisfied it.
must-log:Linted app=cluster-info .*side=base linter=lint#1 command=scripts/lint-kyverno\.sh status=ok lines=0
must-log:Linted app=cluster-info .*side=target linter=lint#1 command=scripts/lint-kyverno\.sh status=ok lines=0
